"# Contributing to Nodus\n\nThis guide covers work on the library itself: adding a component, changing one, fixing a defect.\nAn application built with Nodus does not need it: it starts from the\n[starter](#/guides/starter). The full rules, with their reasons, are in the\n[conventions](#/reference/guidelines) and the [architecture](#/reference/specs) (in French); this\nguide gives the order of work.\n\n## 1. Set up the repository\n\n```bash\nnpm install          # also enables the push guard (core.hooksPath = starter/.githooks)\nnpm test             # unit tests (Vitest)\nnpm run build        # guards, tests, then dist/\n```\n\n`dist/` is what an application receives: build after every change. The build fails if a test is\nred, if interface text is hard-coded, if a referenced token does not exist or if a component has\nno documentation page.\n\n## 2. Pick the component's shape\n\n| Shape | When | Files |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pattern A** | Rendering only, no client-side JavaScript (button, badge, card). | `<name>.js`, `<name>.css` |\n| **Pattern B** | Interaction handled by the component (dialog, tabs, select). | + `<name>-behavior.js` |\n\nA component whose content depends on application state is not a new Pattern B: the Reef recipes\n(lists, reactive island) cover it. The guide\n[Interactive components and state](#/guides/patterns-choice) explains the difference.\n\nOne implementation per family: a new menu item extends `components/menu/`, a field goes through\n`Field`, an overlay extends `OverlayBehavior`. Extend, never copy.\n\n## 3. Write the component\n\nIn `ui-lib/components/<name>/`:\n\n- the template returns an HTML string; same props, same markup (no random id or key);\n- `attrs` is serialised by `attrsToString()`, and the passed class is merged, never duplicated;\n- all visible text goes through `t('key')`, with the key in `core/i18n.js` **and** `core/i18n-en.js`;\n- every CSS value goes through a token (`var(--…)`); the CSS writes no `@layer`, the layer is set\n  on import;\n- a Pattern B carries `data-component` and `reef-ignore`; its behavior registers in the registry.\n\nThen export it from `ui-lib/index.js` and import its CSS in `ui-lib/tokens/index.css`, in its\ntier's section.\n\n## 4. The JSDoc is the documentation\n\nThe site and `dist/API.md` read the JSDoc block right above the function: description, `@since`,\n`@status`, every prop with its description, `@tokens`, `@accessibility`, `@keyboard`, `@event`.\nEvery `@example` is shown **and run** by the tests: it must work as written.\n\nA block separated from its function by a line is no longer read: the function loses its props in\nthe documentation, and a test reports it.\n\n## 5. Document it on the site\n\nFour files, the rest is generated (navigation, search, index):\n\n| File | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `site/catalog.js` | Category, French and English summaries, search synonyms. |\n| `site/content/components/<name>.js` | The page: description, demos, usage, notes, playground. |\n| `site/content/en/components/<name>.js` | English translation, keyed by the French sentence. |\n| `site/mosaic/previews.js` | The mosaic index preview, with the component's states. |\n\nDemos write every sample text in both languages.\n\n## 6. Test\n\n- `tests/fixtures.js` gets the component's minimal props: the generic tests render it and check\n  `attrs` escaping, ARIA, key stability, and the absence of hard-coded text.\n- A critical state or an interaction gets its own test (`tests/components/`, `tests/behaviors/`).\n- A fixed defect starts with the test that proves it: it fails on the old code, then passes.\n- CSS changed: `npm run test:browser` locally (keyboard, snapshots, axe). CI does not run it.\n\n## 7. Record the change\n\n| File | When |\n|---|---|\n| `CHANGELOG.md` | Always: `Added`, `Changed` or `Fixed` in the current version's block. |\n| `SPECS.md` | When a component's API changes, or a test adds a guarantee. |\n| `GUIDELINES.md` | When a contribution convention changes. |\n| `audit_Design_System/results/issues.md` | When an accessibility or quality defect is fixed. |\n\nThe version number goes up when a work cycle opens, with its dated block at the top of the\nCHANGELOG. [What's new](#/reference/whats-new) and [Releases](#/reference/changelog) are how\nit reads.\n\n## 8. Publish\n\n```bash\nnpm run ship         # pushes the branch (never git push)\nnpm run finish       # PR, wait for CI, merge, back to main\n```\n\n`npm run ship` dates unpublished commits at push time; `npm run finish` does not merge if CI\nfails.\n"